<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Chikulamayembe State</h1><p class="st-meta">1780 CE – 1855 CE (approx.) · capital Bolero</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Tumbuka kingdom of northern Malawi founded by ivory traders from east of Lake Malawi, destroyed by Ngoni invaders in the 1850s.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tumbuka-people">Tumbuka</a> tradition recalls that in the late 18th century traders known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balowoka">Balowoka</a> crossed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-malawi">Lake Malawi</a> from the east seeking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>. Their leader settled near Bolero and gained authority over local Tumbuka clans, founding the Chikulamayembe dynasty.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Chikulamayembe built power on control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> trade towards the lake and the coast, distributing imported cloth and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a> to secure the loyalty of clan heads. Authority over the scattered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tumbuka-people">Tumbuka</a> population remained loose, and the state relied on trade rather than a strong military.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the 1850s the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngoni">Ngoni</a> under the successors of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zwangendaba">Zwangendaba</a> invaded northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malawi">Malawi</a> and destroyed the kingdom, incorporating many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tumbuka-people">Tumbuka</a>. The British later restored the Chikulamayembe title as a chieftaincy in 1907.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1780 CE – 1855 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Central and Southern Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:95.12%;width:1.49%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1780 CE – 1855 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bolero</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Traditional Tumbuka religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Chitumbuka</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/malawi">Malawi</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Tumbuka kingdom, Chikulamayembe</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1818&amp;focus=chikulamayembe-state" aria-label="Chikulamayembe State on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5601 2676 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Chikulamayembe State lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5932 2859h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5921 2932h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5921 2932h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Malawi. <a href="/atlas?year=1818&amp;focus=chikulamayembe-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

